From 6584ccd4c3f893a0c5b62a345d9f5f2ca32fc803 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: William Floyd <git@notmy.space>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 17:44:44 +0000
Subject: software_isp: HDR from frames of alternating exposure

While the DRC holds the exposure well below the target and a longer
exposure fits the frame, ask for the exposure alternately short (the
DRC's) and long (the target's, the DRC gain times it), with the statistics
frames kept on the short ones so that the loop works as it does without. The
pass that bins the cells merges each frame's cells with the estimate the
previous ones made, in the short exposure's units, by their inverse
variances from the noise model, leaving out saturated cells and parts that
moved, so that the shadows come from the clean long exposure while the
tone curve and the DRC lift are the ones they were. The previous cells are
also brought to this frame's units when the exposure changed, for the
temporal filter without HDR.

The ratio of the long exposure to the short is no longer the DRC gain,
which the vendor's tone mapping limits to about 2.4, but how much headroom
the brightest pixels need, up to 8: the long exposure is the one the target
asks for, the short one has the same gain and as much less exposure. The
merge, half-float so that its shadows keep their precision, is scaled up
by the ratio to the long exposure's units in the main pass, where
what is brighter than a knee is compressed into the range that is left,
keeping the colour, and the tone curve is the plain one, without the DRC's
lift. HDR starts when the ratio wanted stays over 2 and stops after it has
stayed below 1.6 for 30 frames.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
